    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "THE INTERESTING NEIGHBOUR IS THE ONE THAT FAILS. This fact holds because binary32 dominates binary16 on BOTH axes: 24 significand bits against 11, and an exponent range that strictly contains binary16's. Drop either domination and the property dies -- see F:fp16-bf16-roundtrip-not-identity, where bfloat16 has the wider range but only 8 significand bits, and 83.6% of binary16 values do not survive the trip. The pair is the point: \"cast to a 16-bit format and back\" is not one operation with one answer, and which 16-bit format is chosen decides it.\n\nROUTE. `smt-clausal`, not `smt-term-level`: axeyum returned a DRAT refutation through CNF and re-checked it. The input domain is only 16 bits, but the fpa2bv lowering of a widen-then-narrow miter is far past the 20-bit `TERM_LEVEL_CERT_BITS` budget, so axeyum's own exhaustive-evaluation certificate is not available. The exhaustive route in the second evidence row is LLVM's APFloat, deliberately not axeyum's IR evaluator.\n\nSCOPE. Exhaustive at binary16: every one of the 2^16 inputs was enumerated, and the solver route settles the same 2^16 symbolically. No claim is made for any other pair of formats.",
